  "answer": "`make build`, `make unit` and `make component` all pass now. It took one fix, on the second attempt.\n\n**Final status**\n- `make build`: 0 errors. The 26 warnings are NuGet vulnerability notices for package dependencies (Scriban.Signed, SQLitePCLRaw, System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs), not build problems.\n- `make unit`: 272 of 272 passed.\n- `make component`: 20 of 20 passed.\n\n**What failed**\nOn the first run, 39 of 272 unit tests failed. Almost all were in `SemanticMatcherTests` and `ResultMatcherTests`, failing with errors like `ArgumentException: Unknown semantic matcher 'isnull' for field 'deletedAt'`. Any test using a named matcher (`isUuid`, `isNull`, `greaterThan(...)`, `hasLength(...)`, or a custom matcher) failed this way.\n\nOne more test, `HttpReadinessProbeTests.TryProbe_ServerReturns500_ReturnsFalse`, failed on the first run but passed on the rerun. I didn't change anything related to it and didn't look into it further. It's probably timing- or port-sensitive, so it may fail intermittently.\n\n**Root cause**\nIn `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs`, `ParseSpec` converted the matcher name to lowercase (`spec.ToLowerInvariant()`). The built-in matchers are stored under camelCase names (`isUuid`, `isNull`, \u2026) and looked up case-sensitively, as are custom matchers in `SuiteConfig.CustomMatchers`. So `isUuid` became `isuuid`, nothing matched, and every named matcher threw \"unknown matcher\". The tests and docs all use camelCase names and none expect case-insensitive matching, so the lowercasing was the bug.\n\n**What was changed**\nI removed the two `.ToLowerInvariant()` calls in `ParseSpec` (`src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs:85` and `:91`), so matcher names are passed through as written. I didn't touch any tests, and nothing was committed.",
